{"answer":"There are 7 subsets:\n{1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {1,2,6}, {4,5}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.\n\nFor example, {1,8} has sum 9. The list is exhaustive by partitioning each eligible subset according to its unique largest element m. Necessarily m is 4, 5, 6, 7, or 8; the residual sums 9-m yield respectively 1, 2, 2, 1, and 1 possibilities, for a total of 7. The two supplied approaches agree up to element order and use the same valid partition; the ungrouped-listing route is correctly rejected because it supplies no exhaustiveness argument.","approaches":[{"description":"Partition each eligible subset by its unique largest element and enumerate subsets of smaller elements with the required residual sum.","id":"largest-element-cases","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This gives disjoint cases, excludes all other largest elements, and enumerates every residual-sum possibility."},{"description":"List candidate subsets without a partition or exclusion argument.","id":"ungrouped-listing","outcome":"rejected","reason":"A bare list does not establish that no additional subset has sum 9."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question asks: \"How many subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9?\"","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"target-family","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Let F be the family of subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in steps largest-range through case-four.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["target-family"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"complete-list","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The members of F are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {1,2,6}, {4,5}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["largest-range","case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four","partition"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by summing the disjoint case counts in step total-count.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["complete-list"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"count-seven","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets have sum 9.","step_ids":["total-count"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Verified in step example.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["complete-list"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is one requested subset with sum 9.","step_ids":["example"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the unique-largest-element partition and the complete case enumeration in step partition.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["complete-list"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"exhaustiveness","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The displayed list is exhaustive and contains no duplicates.","step_ids":["partition"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":["target-family"],"id":"largest-range","justification":"A member of F is nonempty and has a largest element m. If m is at most 3, its sum is at most 1+2+3=6, contradicting sum 9; since m belongs to the given ground set, m is at most 8.","statement":"For every member of F, its largest element m lies in {4,5,6,7,8}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-eight","justification":"The elements below 8 must sum to 1, and the only subset of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7} with sum 1 is {1}.","statement":"The m=8 case contributes exactly {1,8}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-seven","justification":"The elements below 7 must sum to 2, and the only subset of {1,2,3,4,5,6} with sum 2 is {2}.","statement":"The m=7 case contributes exactly {2,7}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-six","justification":"The elements below 6 must sum to 3. The only subsets of {1,2,3,4,5} with sum 3 are {3} and {1,2}.","statement":"The m=6 case contributes exactly {3,6} and {1,2,6}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-five","justification":"The elements below 5 must sum to 4. The only subsets of {1,2,3,4} with sum 4 are {4} and {1,3}.","statement":"The m=5 case contributes exactly {4,5} and {1,3,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-four","justification":"The elements below 4 are drawn from {1,2,3} and must sum to 5; only {2,3} does so.","statement":"The m=4 case contributes exactly {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range","case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four"],"id":"partition","justification":"Every nonempty finite subset has one unique largest element. Thus the five largest-element cases are mutually disjoint and, by largest-range, cover F; the preceding case steps enumerate each case completely.","statement":"The five cases form a complete, disjoint partition of F."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four","partition"],"id":"total-count","justification":"The five disjoint cases have counts 1, 1, 2, 2, and 1, whose sum is 7.","statement":"|F|=1+1+2+2+1=7."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"example","justification":"1+8=9, and both elements belong to the stated ground set.","statement":"{1,8} belongs to F."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
